From: freeman@maspar.com (Jay R. Freeman)
Date: Fri, 28 Jan 94 17:20:40 -0800
Subject: [#94-1-665] Magic

The First Extropian Squirrel recalls Arthur C. Clark's third law, more or
less "a sufficiently advanced technology will be indistinguishable from magic
by those who do not understand it", and proposes a variant:  "A sufficiently
unconventional technology will be labeled as magic by those who would rather
not believe it works."  Humans often are much more difficult to convince of
things that lie outside their notions of of how things ought to be, than of
things that fit those paradigms -- they tend to require much more evidence
before accepting items in the former category than in the latter.

Just how to deal with the possibility and prospect of paradigm shift, both as
zealous expounder of the new order and as suspicious potential convert from
the old, is an epistemological problem which -- in the Squirrel's opinion --
most humans have insufficiently addressed.

(A reference may be relevant.  Thomas Kuhn's book, _The_Nature_of_Scientific_
_Revolutions_ (that might be "Structure", not "Nature" -- the Squirrel's copy
is at home) deals explicitly with paradigm shifts in the context of science.
I found it thought-provoking but rather dry.)

                                   -- Jay Freeman, First Extropian Squirrel

