From: Dani Eder <eder@blkbox.COM>
Date: Fri, 28 Jan 1994 21:17:54 -0600 (CST)
Subject: [#94-1-669] Physics - How closed is the Earth as a system

> 

Some posts have been made recently claiming that the Earth is a closed
system.  I would  like to examine this issue from a physics point of
view.  From an energetic point of view, even the most rabid environmentalist
has to agree that the Earth is an open system - without energy input
from the Sun and re-radiation of heat back to space, life as we know
it would end in a matter of days.

Now, let us consider the matter part of us being in a closed system.
Again - even a rabid environmentalist must agree that meteroids and
interplanetary spacecraft show that the Earth is not a perfectly
closed system.  Instead, we should look at what time scale the Earth
is a 'mostly closed' system.  In the natural state, with a mass influx
of around 100 tons per day from meteoroids, we gain an inch of new
material every billion years, which I would call mostly closed.

On the other hand, with human activity, we can reach a much different
answer.  Consider the sunlight falling on the Earth.  It amounts to
350 Watts on average for each square meter of the planet's surface.
In the course of one day, a total of 30 Megajoules falls on that
area (on average).  It turns out that this is just about the energy
required to lift one kilogram to orbit (at perfect efficiency).

Let us define the 'biosphere' as being the top 200 tons per square
meter of the Earth's surface.  This is the atmosphere plus the
top 190 meters of ocean, and about 60 meters down on land.  Pretty
much all living things are found in this region (aside from a few
deep ocean creatures).  If we put our minds to it, we could strip
the biosphere in 200,000 days (550 years) using the sunlight falling
on it.  Conversely, at the same rate we could import new material
equal in mass to the biosphere from elsewhere in the solar system.

Thus, over a time period of half a millenium, I would call the
system 'open', just accounting for the natural solar flux falling
on the Earth, used by humans for biosphere additions or subtractions.

Now, we are not physically limited to just using the energy falling
on the Earth itself.  If we fill a disk as large as the Moon's orbit
with a cloud of solar collectors, we have 3600 times as much energy
to work with.  In this case we could strip the biosphere in under
2 months, and dismantle the entire planet in a mere 3000 years.
Note, I am not advocating doing this - I am merely showing that given
sufficient utilization of the energy the Sun puts through the
Earth's neighborhood, you get a physically open system on time
scales that are pretty short.

So, I conclude that those who claim the Earth is a 'closed system'
are simply wrong from a physics standpoint.

Dani Eder


