> disrupt the environment too.

Thermal sea the least, though.  I dislike centralized power sources
on principle, but in some places they may be necessary.  Being "off the
grid" gives you a tremendous comparative freedom -- with wireless telephone
technology in all areas coming soon there will be no reason to need it.

>   However, the environmental lobby would eventually start to dislike
> decentralized solar power. Why? Because they couldn't control how much
> power people consumed.

There is entirely too much stereotyping going on in this group.  I
worked for CalPIRG for an entire summer and found the people to be
extraordinarily self-reliant in their attitudes.  Socialists are often
very wary of environmentalists because of the socialists' desire for
lots of state-controlled big industry.

>   First of all, the so-called waste-disposal problem isn't really a 
> problem. The amount of waste generated yearly in the _world_ would
> fit in a single warehouse.

If in its original form, but you can't keep it from contaminating the
matter around it (unless that matter is a big lead shield or similar),
which then becomes a disposal problem too, like the several hundred
radioactive frozen beagles in Davis, not to mention the tons of
radioactive frozen beagle shit.

>   Fission stinks anyway. You overlook _REAL SOLAR POWER_ and that's
> Nuclear Fusion.

No, I don't.  I think it's one of our only hopes for the future, and 
agree with your [deleted] analysis.

> > We can learn that it is possible to live

Egg on my face here.  I never finished this #$*&&*! sentence, since I 
tend to write nonlinearly and the response was so long I never got back
to it.  I'd imagine I meant something like "...live a peaceful life of
productive leisure, add in some current technology to make it even
easier and more fun, and be relatively sure that we won't need to throw
away our paradigms again real soon."

>   Nothing is sustainable. Our behavior has never been constant. You are
> making backwards predictions based on our current production and
> resource use. Those predictions are bound to fail. Don't you understand
> what "Singularity" means? It's the central concept of this list.
> The way we live isn't currently sustainable, but we won't continue
> to live the way we do.

I have said many times: I bring up these issues in an attempt to steer
this change in a sustainable direction.  I don't want to see current
deathkultur slowly replaced with something equally stupid.

> Consumption will switch to service and information
> goods. Cars will be used less, not because of trains, but because of
> telecommuting. Production will produce less. Goods will be more
> energy efficient. Tree won't be cut down for paper. Cheap ``clean'' power 
> will be invented. 

Only if we MAKE THESE THINGS HAPPEN!  Your actions determine whether they
will.  If you refuse to buy wasteful products and buy efficient ones
instead, they will.  If you don't use paper, trees won't be cut down.
If you invent cleaner power (!) and use less of it, pollution won't be such
a problem.

> You have absolutely no vision. 

I didn't feel it necessary to explain my vision before making the argument.
Given the incorrect assumptions everyone here seems to have made about
my views, maybe I should have.

>    Those people expanding exponentially will solve their own problems.
> They will die of war, poverty, and disease. "We" (meaning the west),
> are not the population problem. We are a small minority of the world's
> population. Your problem is self-correcting, as long as we don't help
> them and they continue their path. The same with this country. Stop
> subsidizing welfare mothers and they'll stop having babies.

Yes.  But how bad will it get before these problems solve themselves?
What can we do to make sure we're not crushed in the wreckage?  Maybe
we should practice some self-reliant and sustainable techniques so we
don't have to guess as things get worse.

>   If I can't consume, I will have a radically reduced quality of
> life. As far as I'm concerned, your "native cultures in tune with nature"
> have low quality of life, low life expectency, low education, low
> access to information, and no where near the conveinience level I have. 

Read Marshall Sahlins' "The Original Affluent Society" and Bob Black's
commentary (in "Friendly Fire") for some surprising data about quality
of life in such civilizations.

>   You keep talking about sustainable cultures but you have failed
> to present any except for preindustrial ones.

So we gotta make some.

> We won't have any
> leisure time to explore the universe because we won't be able too.
> We'll be more concerned with telling mystical stories of the stars
> around camp fires.

Perhaps I should present my view of one possible society so as to head off
these misconceptions once and for all.  Besides, mystical stories of 
the stars around camp fires are fun to tell, sometimes.

>   If this is the lifestyle you want, why don't you set an example
> by giving up your computer, tv, video gams, stereo, or whatever
> other consumer products you own. I know, I know, you're on a mission
> to save the earth so you need the computer. I say, get rid of it,
> computer production uses hundreds of toxic chemicals.

I do not have a home computer.  I don't need it.  I use mine at work.
I have no television set: it's mindless shit anyway.  I own some used
arcade games that were almost landfill.  I ride my bicycle to work
9 days out of 10.  I don't advocate (how many times have I said this? 
Aaaargh!) total divestiture, but I do walk my talk.  More questions?

> > I believe that drooling over nonexistent technology will get us
> > nowhere, and that we must actively seek ways of life that will allow
> > us the time and tools to explore and invent new technology.  (Hint:
> > That's not working for someone else 60 hours a week.)
> 
>    We need socialism along with the worker's paradise,
> and the "abolition of work"?

Socialism does not enter into my desired worldview, as I have said.
Work is more effective if I work for my own reasons and not because I
have to be there at 9 AM or get a reprimand.  I do my best work when I
don't feel like I'm working.

> Please define these new ways of life
> which will allow us to quickly develop new technology before we
> die of age, disease, or meteor.

I'm working on it.  I don't claim to have solved it, but I feel I have
several pieces of the puzzle.  Watch this space.  I'm extraordinarily
busy but will make some time for this interesting dialogue.

